.TH FKSTRTRUNC 3 2026-09-24 "fkstring" "fkstring Library Manual"
.nh
.SH NAME
fkstrtrunc \- truncate an fkstring
.SH LIBRARY
fkstring library
.RI ( libfkstring ,\~ \-lfkstring )
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <fkstring.h>
.PP
.BI "fkstring *fkstrtrunc(fkstring *" fks ", size_t " newlen );
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR fkstrtrunc ()
shortens
.I fks
to its first
.I newlen
bytes, in place.
If
.I newlen
is not less than
.BR fkstrlen ( fks ),
it does nothing; it never lengthens a string.
Truncating to 0 frees the buffer, leaving an empty
.BR fkstring .
.PP
Otherwise the buffer is shrunk only when it has become much larger than
the new length: more than 350%
.RI ( _deflatefactor )
of it, and more than 16 bytes
.RI ( _minalloc ).
It is then shrunk to
.IR newlen "\~+\~1"
bytes, or 16 if that is smaller.
This hysteresis keeps a string that alternately grows and shrinks from
being reallocated on every call; see
.BR fkstring (3).
.PP
.BR fkremove (3)
and the trim functions truncate through
.BR fkstrtrunc (),
and shrink the buffer by the same rule.
.SH RETURN VALUE
.IR fks ,
or NULL if
.I fks
is NULL.
.SH ERRORS
.BR fkstrtrunc ()
does not return on allocation failure; see
.BR fkstring (3).
.SH NOTES
Shrinking may move the buffer, invalidating pointers into it.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR fkstring (3),
.BR fkremove (3),
.BR fktrim (3),
.BR fkslack (3)
